name: legendary
after: 29-proving-this-machine
intro: The last one before the machine itself falls. Every stage here uses
intro: something from an earlier level, and none of them fit on one line
intro: comfortably. If you can build these with no reference and no tab, you
intro: know this machine.
---
setup: Each level declares the commands it drills on ref: lines. You want the
setup: commands that appear across the most separate levels -- the load-bearing
setup: ones the whole curriculum leans on.
q: print the five most frequently drilled commands with their counts, most first
a: sed -n 's/^ref: //p' /usr/share/learn/levels/* | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -n 5
ref: uniq
---
setup: A ref exists for every shipped command; the levels drill only some. There
setup: is no comm here and ash has no process substitution, so you difference two
setup: lists with grep.
q: write the drilled commands to /tmp/taught, one per line, sorted and deduplicated
a: sed -n 's/^ref: //p' /usr/share/learn/levels/* | sort -u > /tmp/taught
ref: sort
---
setup: /tmp/taught now holds one command per line.
q: print every documented command absent from that list
a: ls /usr/share/learn/ref | grep -vxF -f /tmp/taught
ref: grep
---
setup: Prove nothing in the corpus was altered since you built it: one digest
setup: over every ref file's contents at once.
q: print a single sha256 digest covering every ref file's contents
a: cat /usr/share/learn/ref/* | sha256sum
a: find /usr/share/learn/ref -type f | sort | xargs cat | sha256sum
ref: sha256sum
---
setup: The final question. /proc/mounts, field 4 the comma-separated options.
q: print each mount option that appears on more than one filesystem, with its count
a: awk '{print $4}' /proc/mounts | tr ',' '\n' | sort | uniq -c | awk '$1>1'
ref: awk
